window.docContents[79].resolve({"/Overview/#overview":{"contents":"\n\nThis section showcases various features of cvc.lean for new users to get an idea of what it can do.\nIt is light on technical details which are provided in the rest of this manual. For now just know\nthat cvc.lean has two APIs that differ in how Terms (and a few other types) are encoded and the\nstatic guarantees they provide:\n\n* Cvc.Typed strongly-types terms so that the cvc5-level sort of a term appears in its type, making\n  ill-formed terms (and most solver queries) fail at compile-times. For instance:* Term Bool, Term String, etc.* asserting a formula in a solver takes a Term Bool.* Cvc.Untyped just has a simple type Term for terms and you are responsible for creating\n  well-formed terms and querying the solver correctly; failure to do so will result in a runtime\n  error.\n\nThe examples below use the typed API.\n\nopen Cvc Typed\n\n\nAlso, we need an instance of type-class Ω in scope to manipulate sorts, terms, solvers, etc.\nThink of it as a \"cvc5 world\" which we need to do anything, in particular to write code in\ncvc.lean's Env monad.\n\nvariable [Ω]\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Cvc.lean -- High-Level CVC5 API","header":"1. Overview","id":"/Overview/#overview"}});